1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,600
Margaret Wilson is selling the trucking company / she built over **thirty-five years**. / Her family has never talked about / what's **fair**.

2
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:19,600
Most families don't, / until the money arrives. / By then the advisor has heard from **one person**, / and more than **half of heirs** / don't keep their parents' advisor.

3
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:27,600
Family Table helps **both sides** of the sale: / Margaret's family, / and Alex, / her PNC advisor.

4
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:37,600
PNC already **banks the business**. / Payments to deal advisers flag a likely sale / **a year ahead**, / so Alex calls Margaret / **before** anything is signed.

5
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:45,600
Margaret previews what a sale could mean, / sets her goals, / and invites her family. / Each of them answers **in private**.

6
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:54,600
Claire writes what she's never told her mother: / equal is the only fair split. / **She** decides / whether Alex can read it.

7
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:06,600
Alex now hears **four voices**, not one. / AI reads the check-ins, / scores the gaps, / and finds the old will. / Margaret thought the kids agreed. / The widest gap is **fairness**, / and **Alex approves** the brief.

8
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:15,600
Sam sees **his number** under every split, / and votes **in private**. / Three choices / turn twelve possible plans / into **one**.

9
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:23,600
When Sam has a question, / it goes to **Alex**. / AI drafts the reply; / Alex checks it, / and sends.

10
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:32,600
One year on, / the sale has closed, / the plan is everyday banking, / and **all five** Wilsons work with PNC.

11
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:47,600
Why only PNC? / It **sees** the sale coming, / **hears** what families won't say out loud, / and **meets the heirs** before the money moves. / **Family Table.** / A plan the family trusts.

